00) what is Kubernetics 
  Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform used to automate the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications.
                                 or
  Kubernetes is used to deploy, manage, scale, and monitor containerized applications automatically.
    Orchestration = automatically managing many containers.

01) Why do we need Kubernetes?
    We use Kubernetes to automate the deployment, scaling, networking, and recovery of containerized applications.
     It is especially useful when we have many containers running across multiple servers.

02) Docker vs Kubernetes
    Docker runs containers. Kubernetes manages containers.
        __________________________________________________________________________________________
        |  Docker	                                  |   Kubernetes                                |
        __________________________________________________________________________________________
        |  Creates/runs containers	                |  Manages containers                         |
        |  Good for running individual containers   | 	Good for managing many containers         |
        |  Builds images using Dockerfile	          |  Uses container images                      |
        |  docker run starts a container	          |  Kubernetes deploys/manages containers      |
        |  docker ps shows running containers	      |  kubectl get pods shows running Pods        |
        ___________________________________________________________________________________________

      Docker is used to build and run containerized applications, while Kubernetes is used to orchestrate and manage containers, 
       including scaling, load balancing, deployment, and recovery.

03) Cluster, Node, and Pod
        1. Cluster
              A Kubernetes Cluster is the complete Kubernetes environment.
        2. Node
              A Node is a machine inside the Kubernetes cluster.
              It can be a physical machine or virtual machine.
              Node = Machine/server where your application can run.
        3. Pod 
              This is very important.
              A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
              Usually, a Pod contains one main application container.

        Easy way to remember 🧠
        Cluster = Complete Kubernetes environment
        Node = Machine inside the cluster
        Pod = Smallest deployable unit that runs your container(s)
        Container = Your actual packaged application

04) Control Plane vs Worker Node
          Cluster → Nodes → Pods → Containers
          But who controls all these Nodes and Pods?
            Kubernetes mainly has two sides:          
                Control Plane = manages the cluster 🧠
                Worker Nodes = run your applications 💻

          A Kubernetes cluster consists of nodes. Nodes are machines that run workloads, and Pods are the smallest deployable 
           units in Kubernetes that contain one or more containers.

            
            1. Control Plane
              ---------------
                    Think of the Control Plane as the brain of Kubernetes.
                    It decides things like:                    
                        Where should a Pod run?
                        Is a Pod running correctly?
                        Did a Pod crash?
                        Do we need more Pods?
                        Which Worker Node should run the application?
            
                    So remember:                
                        Control Plane = controls and manages the Kubernetes cluster.                    
                        We'll learn its components like API Server, Scheduler, Controller Manager, and etcd separately later.
                        
            2. Worker Node
            --------------
                  A Worker Node is the machine where your actual application workloads run.                  
                  For example, your Spring Boot application:                      
                      Worker Node → Pod → Container → Spring Boot application                      
                      A cluster can have multiple Worker Nodes.
                      
                  Simple real-world example
                      Think of a restaurant:                      
                          👨‍💼 Control Plane = Manager
                          👨‍🍳 Worker Nodes = Workers
                          📦 Pods = Work assigned to workers                      
                      The manager decides what needs to happen and where.                      
                      The workers actually perform the work.

                  Important difference
                  __________________________________________________________
                 |   Control Plane         |          	Worker Node          |
                 |  Manages the cluster	   |     Runs application workloads  |
                 |   Makes decisions       |    	Follows those decisions    |
                 |   Schedules Pods	       |     Hosts Pods                  |
                  __________________________________________________________
                Monitors the cluster	Runs containers
